# THE BUILD, NOT THIS FILE, IS NOW WHAT GUARANTEES LF IN THE PUBLISHED BYTES.
# build.py's lf() folds CRLF to LF in every text byte it writes into dist/ -- the
# hashed scripts, the copied stylesheets, the carried blobs and the 253 rendered
# pages alike -- and hashes the LF form, so the working-tree line endings of the
# sources no longer reach the output at all. That closes the hole this rule was
# holding open with one finger:
#
#   * The published bytes now equal the built bytes. dist/ is committed into
#     cf-live, cf-live has no .gitattributes of its own, and this machine has
#     core.autocrlf=true system-wide, so `git add -A` used to renormalise on the
#     way into the index. Measured on 8fc64aad, that quietly rewrote two of the
#     607 files: assets/site.f14bf457.js 3,801 -> 3,704 bytes and
#     assets/fonts.css 6,560 -> 6,407. A dist/ that is already LF cannot be
#     rewritten, so the tree the preflight certifies is the tree Pages serves.
#
#   * The build stopped being machine-local. site.js was hashed from its
#     working-tree bytes, which are CRLF here and LF on any autocrlf=false clone;
#     one commit therefore built site.f14bf457.js on Windows and site.96278e59.js
#     on CI, with a different _headers and 253 different pages behind each.
#
# THE SCOPING ARGUMENT THIS FILE USED TO MAKE WAS RIGHT, AND HAS NOW BEEN ACTED
# ON DELIBERATELY. It said that pulling site.js and the CSS bundle sources into
# this rule "changes their bytes, therefore the bundle hash, therefore every
# page, and that is a content change for the owner to approve rather than a fix
# to smuggle in beside a beacon". It is exactly that content change, made on
# purpose and named in the runbook: /assets/site.f14bf457.js becomes
# /assets/site.96278e59.js, and all 253 pages plus _headers move with it. The
# bundle hash does NOT move (bundle.07f23879.css both before and after), because
# css_bundle_content() has always read its six sources in text mode, where
# Python's universal newlines had already been doing lf()'s job.
#
# The rule below is now belt-and-braces rather than the only guard, and it is
# kept for two reasons: it keeps assets/cw-events.js byte-identical to the
# /js/cw-events.js cf-live serves today (blob and working tree both 6,080 bytes,
# so the hash is cw-events.853632c8.js on every platform), and it documents at
# the file that the beacon is a verbatim carry rather than something this repo
# authors. Do not widen it to the other assets: normalising them is build.py's
# job now, and a second mechanism doing the same thing is a second thing to keep
# in agreement.
/assets/cw-events.js -text
